      "body": "Adds one entry to the Developer Tooling section, in alphabetical order (`dsh-tool-approval` ? `dsh-triage` ? `dsh-verify`).\n\n`dsh-triage` answers two questions a profile owner actually has: \"should I install this?\" (declaration check plus an isolated measurement) and \"why did my plugin disappear?\" (row-level admission, import failures, declared-but-unregistered capabilities). It is read-only by design: no install/remove, no credential access, no security claims. It also reports what a *target* DSH build would deny (`upgrade --to <version>`), which is what we used to sanity-check the compatibility note below.\n\n- Repo: https://github.com/jjjjjjjjnnjnn/dsh-triage (MIT)\n- DSH compatibility: `>=0.2.0-rc.1 <0.3.0-0` (measured on `0.2.1-rc.4`; this is the range declared in `packages/plugin/package.json#engines.dsh`)\n- Published on npm as `dsh-triage` (0.2.0), so the link and the install path both resolve\n- Source-level DSH integration: `packages/plugin` is a profile bundle (`package.json#dsh.bundle.patch` ? `cordis.patch.yml` with `insert:` rows) that registers a read-only HTTP route and a `compat_verdict` tool through the documented Cordis seams\n- Publicly accessible, actively maintained; links verified on the day of submission\r\n",
